## Types

The engine's node schemas (`nodeBase`, `NodeStyle`, ports, endpoints, layers and every node type not tied to the
diagram vocabulary) are defined in `@dxos/scene` ([org.dxos.scene#D-90]); the View, registry and projection types
are react-ui-canvas's. They are plain Effect schemas, so a host wraps them into an ECHO type without changing a field
(decision 3).

```mdl
type View
  desc: >
    One container's contents: nodes and links keyed by id in a single flat coordinate space (decision 4).
    The same record holds a View (`kind` absent or `view`) and an Object definition (`kind:
    'composite'`). Bounds are NEVER stored — they are derived from the content, padded and grown to the
    major grid (decision 6), so a View has no edges to run out of. `createViewSchema(nodes)` composes the
    schema from a registry's node schemas; `OpenView` is the same over `NodeBase`, which is what the
    engine itself can validate for a host it does not know the types of.
  fields:
    id: ViewId
    name?: string
    nodes: Record<NodeId, Node>
    links: Record<LinkId, Link>
    kind?: ViewKind                   # 'view' | 'composite' (an Object definition)
    anonymous?: boolean               # an Object made by Group, kept out of the library until named or placed again
    layers?: Record<LayerId, Layer>   # replaced by document Tags (planned)
```

```mdl
type Layer
  desc: >
    A View's layer, as built: elements paint by their layer's `z`, then by their own, and a hidden layer
    is neither drawn nor hit. A View with no layers has one, implicitly. Tags replace it (planned):
    the layers migrate to tags on load, and drawing order moves to the container order.
  fields:
    id: LayerId
    name: string
    z: string
    hidden?: boolean
```

```mdl
type Tag
  desc: >
    (planned) A document-wide label. An element is hidden or locked when any of its tags is; its
    opacity is the product of its tags' opacities and its own. A tag on an Object applies to every
    placement of it; hiding one placement is an override, not a tag. A tag never orders anything.
  fields:
    id: TagId
    name: string
    hidden?: boolean
    locked?: boolean
    opacity?: number
```

```mdl
type NodeBase
  desc: >
    What every node is, whatever its type adds (decision 1). The frame is `size` centred on `center`;
    everything the engine does — hit testing, ports, resize, marquee, portal mapping — reads only these
    fields, which is why a host type is a first-class citizen rather than a special case. `z` is a
    fractional order key (`utils/order.ts`), shared with links: one namespace, one selection, one undo
    log, one `update` intent for both.
  fields:
    id: NodeId
    type: NodeType                    # a built-in (below) or a host's own
    z: string                         # fractional order key: the node's place in its container's order
    locked?: boolean                  # no intent moves, resizes or deletes it
    center: Point
    size: Size
    rotation?: number                 # degrees clockwise about the centre
    ports?: Port[]                    # absent: the type's definition supplies them (decision 12)
    layer?: LayerId
    class?: string                    # a style class: the element's named `style`
    style?: NodeStyle                 # the element's `overrides`
    tags?: TagId[]                    # (planned)
```

```mdl
type BuiltinNode
  desc: >
    The engine's own types, each `NodeBase` plus its own fields; `isRectNode`-style guards narrow to
    them, sound because the registry maps one type name to one schema. A host's types (a UML class, a
    compute shape) are `NodeBase` to the engine and render through their own `NodeDef.component`.
  literals: RectNode | EllipseNode | CloudNode | PolygonNode | PolylineNode | NoteNode | ViewNode | InstanceNode | PathNode | GeneratorNode | DiagramNode
  fields:
    RectNode: { label?: string }
    EllipseNode: { label?: string }       # inscribed in the frame; one port per side, at its centre
    NoteNode: { text: string }
    ViewNode: { scene: ViewId, link? }    # opens the View it names, drawn live inside its frame; stored `frame` reads as `view`
    InstanceNode: { composite: ViewId, overrides?, fit?: 'fit' | 'fill' | 'none' }   # places an Object
    PathNode: { d: string, viewBox: Bounds, fill? }
    GeneratorNode: { generator: string, params?, seed? }   # docs/ANIMATION.md §6
```

```mdl
type NodeStyle
  desc: >
    Presentation a node carries; every field optional, the frame supplying the default look. `hue` is a
    theme hue colouring fill, text and border together (never a raw colour), so a View stays themed in
    both modes.
  fields:
    hue?: string                      # a theme hue, annotated for the Form's hue picker
    rounded?: boolean
    fill?: boolean
    border?: boolean
    guide?: boolean                   # dashed and unfilled: an annotation, not content
    fontSize?: number                 # text size in the node's own scene units; overrides the canvas's class
    className?: string                # a host's own look
```

```mdl
type Link
  desc: >
    A typed edge: `line` (straight), `curve` (cubic Bézier leaving each port along its side's normal),
    `spline` (the polyline through its control `points`, each interior point rounded by a quadratic that
    takes the point as its control, so a corner reads as a corner) or `smart` (routed rather than drawn:
    a stub leaves each port along its normal by half a major cell and one segment joins them, rounded the
    same way — nothing stored, so the route follows the nodes). A link that names no type is `smart`, and
    fixtures and generated diagrams draw smart links. A link is NOT a node with a special kind — node-only
    operations never have to filter links out (decision 9).
  fields:
    id: LinkId
    type: 'line' | 'curve' | 'spline' | 'smart'
    z: string
    locked?: boolean
    source: Endpoint
    target: Endpoint
    directed?: boolean                # shorthand for ends: { end: 'arrow' }
    ends?: { start?: Marker, end?: Marker }    # 'arrow' | 'circle'; replaces what directed implies
    points?: Point[]                  # spline only, in order
```

```mdl
type Endpoint
  desc: >
    Either end of a link, and the reason a link can exist before both its ends do: `{node, port?}` with
    no port pairs automatically (the closest appropriate pair, recomputed on every projection, so a
    moved node re-routes), and `{point}` is a free end at a scene point (decision 3) — what a link tool
    dragged on empty canvas draws, and what dragging an end off a node leaves behind.
  literals: PortEndpoint | PointEndpoint
  fields:
    PortEndpoint: { node: NodeId, port?: PortId }
    PointEndpoint: { point: Point }
```

```mdl
type Port
  desc: >
    An attachment point on a node's frame. Ports come from the type, not the instance (decision 12);
    `portsPerSide` spreads them along each side (never at a corner) and they draw at the major grid line
    nearest each offset, so a link lands on a line rather than near one. `accepts` constrains which end
    of a link may land here, and a link between two constrained ports is directed by that alone.
  fields:
    id: PortId                        # '<side><index>', e.g. 'e2'
    side: 'n' | 'e' | 's' | 'w'
    offset: number                    # 0..1 along that side
    accepts?: 'in' | 'out' | 'any'    # default any
```

```mdl
type Camera
  desc: >
    The canvas's position over one View: screen = (scene + {x, y}) * zoom. Per-canvas state in an atom,
    never in the model (two canvases on one View stay independent), clamped to MIN_ZOOM..MAX_ZOOM (1/32,
    32). Coordinates are per-View and local (decision 5) — a global (x, y, depth) compounds float error
    through the compositor after a few levels.
  fields:
    x: number
    y: number
    zoom: number
```

```mdl
type NodeDef
  desc: >
    A node type's definition: its schema, what it renders, where its ports are, how it may be
    manipulated, and how the palette presents it. Per type, never per instance, so the data stays small
    and ports come for free. A host composes its registry (and with `createViewSchema` its View
    schema) from the built-ins and its own — this is the whole of what "open node types" means.
  fields:
    type: NodeType
    name: string
    icon: string
    key?: string                      # palette shortcut; a large registry leaves most without one
    group?: string                    # palette group
    schema: Schema.Codec<NodeBase>
    component: Component<NodeViewProps>
    create: (props: { id, z, center, size }) => Node
    defaultSize: Size
    ports?: (node: Node) => Port[]    # explicit layout; absent, portsPerSide spreads them
    portsPerSide?: number             # default 3; the ellipse takes 1
    resizable?: boolean
    minSize?: Size
    maxSize?: Size
    openable?: boolean                # double-click opens it (a View drills in)
```

```mdl
type ViewStore
  desc: >
    Views and Object definitions by id behind atoms, so a canvas subscribes to exactly the View it
    shows. The seam that lets persistence change without the surface noticing: `createMemoryStore` for
    stories and tests, plugin-canvas's ECHO-backed store for a drawing. Undo reads the store's past
    through `versions` (Automerge heads over ECHO; absent, the atom's own snapshots are the heads).
  fields:
    views: Atom.Writable<Record<ViewId, View>>
    view: (id: ViewId) => Atom<View | undefined>
    styles?: Atom.Writable<StyleMap>  # the drawing's style classes
    isRoot?: (id: ViewId) => boolean  # a root of its own (a linked drawing's), kept when no node opens it
    versions?: VersionedSource        # heads(), read(path, heads), write(path, value)
```

```mdl
type Projection
  desc: >
    The only thing the surface knows about the model (decision 11). It reads a positioned View and
    receives intents; what an intent MEANS is the projection's business — freehand writes the
    coordinates, constrained rewrites them as cardinal constraints, dynamic keeps a graph and positions
    rows from it, compute mirrors creates and links into a compute graph. A projection with a model of
    its own exposes it as `model`, versioned like the store, so undo patches both together.
  fields:
    scene: Atom<View>
    apply: (intent: Intent) => void
    capabilities: Capabilities        # move, resize, link, create, delete, update, layout — each optional
    model?: VersionedSource           # constraints, a graph: absent, the store is the whole model
    restored?: () => void             # after undo or redo, so a mirror (a compute graph) catches up
    constrain?: (intent: Intent) => Intent | undefined    # what apply would make of it, for previews
    lattice?: LatticeSpec
```

```mdl
type CanvasHistory
  desc: >
    The canvas's undo log. Every committed edit is one entry of field-level patches between the store's
    heads before and after it, so undo writes back only what the edit wrote and a concurrent edit to
    another field survives. `createLocalHistory` keeps two stacks in memory; a host with Composer's
    `HistoryTracker` records each commit there instead, so gestures, agents and MCP share one log.
  fields:
    commit: (change: () => void, options?: { mergeKey? }) => boolean   # a commit inside another joins it
    undo: () => boolean
    redo: () => boolean
    state: Atom<{ canUndo, canRedo }>
    connect: () => () => void         # follow the host's history
```

```mdl
type Tool
  desc: The active tool; a node type draws that node, a link type is what a port drag creates.
  literals: { kind: 'select' } | { kind: 'hand' } | { kind: 'node', type } | { kind: 'link', type }
```

## Components

One folder per component under `src/components`, stories beside it, all behind the `src/index.ts`
barrel. Rendering is DOM with the camera as one CSS transform, SVG for the link and control layers
(decision 7); no third-party canvas engine, and d3 only as pure functions (decision 8). The tool rail, display
panel, inspector panel and inspectors are the core's, shared with 3D.

```mdl
component Canvas
  desc: >
    The surface: one View under a camera, with the control frame over it and, when it runs on its own, its tool
    rail, toolbar, breadcrumbs and inspector panels around it. Owns the pointer state machine, the key table and navigation between
    depths; commands live in a React-free `CanvasController` organised by aspect (selection, editing,
    tools, time, navigation, look). Owns NO model — every change leaves through `projection.apply`,
    inside a `history.commit`.
  props:
    store: ViewStore
    root: ViewId                      # the root View
    nodes?: NodeRegistry              # defaults to the built-ins
    links?: LinkRegistry
    createProjection?: (options) => Projection     # per View; freehand by default
    projection?: Projection           # a host-owned one instead, shared with its panels
    history?: CanvasHistory | CreateHistory        # local by default
    atoms?: CanvasAtoms               # externally owned canvas state (two canvases, a persisted camera)
    grid?: number                     # minor spacing in scene px (16)
    readonly?: boolean
    inspectors?: InspectorMode
  state:
    camera: Camera
    path: ViewId[]                    # root to the View being edited; its length is the depth
    isolated?: ViewId                 # the Object open in isolation
    selection: Set<ElementId>         # nodes and links together
    hover, point, tool, linkType, snap, debug, drag, editing, clipboard
  layout: |
    ┌─ scene-view ────────────────────────────────┐
    │ [toolbar]                       [breadcrumbs]│
    │ [tools]                                      │
    │        grid (screen space, camera-fed)       │
    │        scene layer (one CSS transform)       │
    │        link layer (svg, same transform)      │
    │        control frame (svg, same transform)   │
    │        overlay (host's, same transform)      │
    └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

```mdl
component SceneLayer
  desc: >
    The nodes and links of one View in their container order, and — for a `view` node — the child View
    rendered live inside it at the node's scale, which is what makes depth continuous rather than a page
    change; an Instance draws its Object's contents the same way. Below `liveDepth` a View node draws a
    preview instead; one being drilled into renders plain, as the child will look once entered.
  props:
    scene: View
    registry: NodeRegistry
    zoom: number                      # camera zoom × the portal scales above it
    depth: number
    liveDepth: number
```

```mdl
component ControlFrame
  desc: >
    Everything drawn about the selection rather than in the View: the selection outline, eight resize
    handles, the hover border, ports, the rubber band of a link drag, the marquee, a spline's control
    points and the midpoint dots that add one, and the provisional frame a create gesture will land in.
    Handles draw above ports, so a handle wins at a side centre.
  props:
    scene, selection, hover, drag, zoom
    showPorts: boolean                # every node (a link tool) or only the hovered one
    createFrame?: Bounds
  emits:
    onHandlePointerDown, onPortPointerDown, onEndPointerDown, onPointPointerDown
```

```mdl
component Toolbar
  desc: >
    Optional chrome (decision 5): up and breadcrumbs, fit, zoom, snap, undo/redo, cut/copy/paste/delete,
    a create menu, auto layout, debug, and a readout of zoom, pointer and depth. Every label prints its
    chord from `model/keys.ts` rather than naming one.
  props: { actions, nodes, capabilities, snap, debug }
```

```mdl
component Breadcrumbs
  desc: >
    The path from the root View to the View being edited; a click drills out to that level. An Object
    in isolation reads `Objects › <name>`.
  props: { path: ViewId[], store }
```

```mdl
component Grid
  desc: >
    Multi-resolution grid in screen space, fed the camera. Levels are a fourfold apart, from a quarter of
    the minor grid to far past the major one, and a level's line weight follows its ON-SCREEN spacing
    rather than its index among the drawn levels — so the same lines are drawn at the same weight either
    side of a drill-in, which is what makes the swap invisible.
  props: { camera, size }
```

```mdl
component PartEditor
  desc: >
    In-place text editing: a `react-ui-editor` over one `TextPart` of a node (a rect or ellipse label, a
    text body, a class's name, attributes or methods). Enter commits an `update` (Mod-Enter in
    multi-line parts), Escape rejects, blur commits.
  props: { node, part, bounds, onCommit, onCancel }
```

```mdl
component Viewport2D
  desc: >
    The 2D viewport the editor draws (`createViewport2D`): `Canvas.Root` and `Canvas.Viewport` over the open scene's
    2D layouts read as Views. It mirrors the editor's selection, tool, drill path, camera, snap, axes and guides into
    the engine's atoms and the engine's changes back, and turns each store write into intents. As a picture it draws
    transparent and read-only, with no grid, snap, axes, guides or context menu.
  props:
    options: Viewport2DOptions        # the host's node types, mode (projection), generators and live depth
```

```mdl
component CanvasBridge
  desc: >
    The 2D viewport's canvas services for the editor's parts drawn outside it: the Properties section sits beside
    the viewport, not inside its `Canvas.Root`. One per 2D config, so each editor reads its own canvas.
```

## Operations

The projection seam IS the operation surface: these are the only ways the model changes, and every one
of them is a value the view hands to `projection.apply`. `reduceIntent` is freehand's semantics as a
pure reducer, which is what makes them testable without a DOM.

```mdl
op move
  desc: Translate every unlocked node in `ids` by `delta`. The gesture snaps to the MINOR grid as it runs.
  input:
    ids: NodeId[]
    delta: Point
  output: View
```

```mdl
op resize
  desc: >
    Set one unlocked node's frame. Shift keeps the centre, otherwise the opposite edge is fixed;
    `minSize` and `maxSize` come from the type's definition (`utils/resize.ts`).
  input:
    id: NodeId
    bounds: Bounds
  output: View
```

```mdl
op link
  desc: >
    Add a link. Rejected when a node end names a node that does not exist, or when both ends are the
    same node; free ends need nothing, which is why a link tool works on empty canvas.
  input:
    link: Link
  output: View
  errors:
    unknownNode: an end names a node not in the View — the intent is dropped, not partially applied
    selfLink: both ends are the same node
```

```mdl
op create
  desc: Add a node, built by its type's `NodeDef.create`. A new node arrives on the MAJOR grid.
  input:
    node: Node
  output: View
```

```mdl
op delete
  desc: >
    Remove nodes and links by id. A link loses its meaning with either end, so deleting a node deletes
    its links too — the projection does this, never the view.
  input:
    ids: ElementId[]
  output: View
```

```mdl
op reorder
  desc: >
    Move one element in its container's order by setting its fractional `z`; nodes and links share the
    key space. Dragging a row in the Views tree emits it.
  input:
    id: ElementId
    z: string
  output: View
```

```mdl
op layer
  desc: Add a layer, or replace the one with its id (a rename, a visibility toggle, a move in the order).
  input:
    layer: Layer
  output: View
  note: Replaced by tag operations when Tags land (planned).
```

```mdl
op removeLayer
  desc: Remove a layer and every element on it; a View's last layer stays.
  input:
    id: LayerId
  output: View
```

```mdl
op update
  desc: >
    Property edits — a label, a text body, geometry, a spline's control points, a style. `id` and `type`
    are never changed by an update, whatever the values carry.
  input:
    id: ElementId
    values: NodeValues | LinkValues
  output: View
```

```mdl
op batch
  desc: Several intents as ONE model change, so a paste or a drill-in fix-up is one undo step.
  input:
    intents: Intent[]
  output: View
```

```mdl
op layout
  desc: >
    Arrange the View into ranked rows along its links, through `@dxos/diagram`'s ranking — the engine
    the dynamic projection already lays its graph out with, rather than a second one. Unlike the old
    editor's `@antv` layout it keeps every node: type, size and content are untouched and only the
    centre moves. An intent rather than a view action, so a projection that owns its own positions may
    rewrite or refuse it, and a read-only view cannot invoke it.
  input:
    ids?: ElementId[]                 # absent: the whole View
  output: View
  note: >
    A row is as tall as its tallest node and a column as wide as its widest, so mixed sizes do not
    overlap; every centre lands on the major grid; a locked node stays where it is. The toolbar's
    button is enabled by `Capabilities.layout` and lays out the selection when two or more are
    selected, otherwise the board.
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Depth and navigation
  View nesting IS the hierarchy (decision 4); a `view` node renders its child live, so moving between
  levels is a camera move rather than a page change. Below, a _portal_ is a `view` node seen as the
  window it draws its child through.
  req F-1.1: Double-clicking a View drills in; Escape drills out; the breadcrumbs drill to any level.
  req F-1.2: Drill-in stops where the child would pass 1:1, so its text lands at natural size rather
    than magnified to fill the view.
  req F-1.3: Zooming past a portal drills in on its own; zooming out below the arrival coverage drills
    out. Auto-exit measures against the coverage the view ARRIVED with, never an absolute threshold, so
    a capped child (or a frame that shrinks as its first node is drawn) is not thrown straight back out.
  req F-1.4: A portal's frame is the portal box times a power of the grid ratio, never once, placed on
    the major grid scaled by that factor: the child's grid lines land on the parent's own lines, not
    merely at their spacing, so the grids stay aligned through a drill-in.
  req F-1.5: A View entered through a portal keeps the frame it arrived with for the visit and exits
    through that same frame; the root's bounds stay derived.
  req F-1.6: At most `liveDepth` levels mount live; deeper portals draw as previews (decision 10).
  req F-1.7: Alt+Left / Alt+Right walk the canvas's own camera history.
  req F-1.8: An Object is never drilled into: opening it from the Objects panel shows its definition in
    isolation; Escape returns to the View and camera the user left.
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Grid and snapping
  req F-2.1: The grid is drawn only while snapping is on (G toggles both): its lines are what a gesture
    lands on, so drawing them when nothing snaps states a constraint the canvas is not applying.
  req F-2.2: Gestures snap to the FINEST LEVEL THE GRID ACTUALLY DRAWS, read back through the same rule
    the component filters by, so the lines and the snap cannot part company. A unit fixed in scene units
    diverges as soon as the zoom moves: far enough in the drawn lines are finer than the snap, and in a
    nested View — entered at a fraction of the parent's zoom — they are coarser and the snap lands on
    nothing visible.
  req F-2.3: A node drawn on the canvas, moved or resized lands on the major level, so its edges and ports
    stay on major lines; a palette drop, a dragged control point and a press that moves less than half a
    minor cell (a click) snap to the minor one or not at all.
  req F-2.4: An arrow nudges by the minor grid, Shift+arrow by a major cell.
  req F-2.5: A link band follows the pointer exactly and snaps once, as it lands.
  req F-2.6: Snapping off means every gesture is free and the grid is hidden; nothing else changes.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Selection and editing
  req F-3.1: Nodes and links are selected together in one set; Delete removes either.
  req F-3.2: A marquee replaces the selection, Shift adds, Alt subtracts.
  req F-3.3: A port drag creates a link; dropping on empty canvas leaves a free end; dragging an end
    re-attaches it, previewed as created while hovering a valid drop.
  req F-3.4: A selected spline offers the midpoint of every span; pressing one inserts a control point
    there and moves it in the same gesture.
  req F-3.5: A create drag previews the bounds the node will land in; a palette drop shows that frame
    alone, since the pointer already carries the drag's own preview.
  req F-3.6: A box drawn on the canvas is exactly what the pointer swept — no minimum and no fallback to
    the type's default — and a gesture that snapped to nothing creates nothing rather than planting a
    default-sized node under the click.
  req F-3.7: A create with NO drawn box (a palette drop, the toolbar's create menu) takes the type's
    default size divided by the camera zoom, so it covers the same screen area at any zoom and any
    depth. The camera is the right quantity because it is independent of content: the portal's own
    factor grows with the child's bounds, so scaling by it would feed back, each node enlarging the next.
  req F-3.8: Picking a shape or link tool clears the selection; the node the gesture makes becomes it.
  req F-3.9: Double-click edits a node's text part in place; Enter commits, Escape rejects.
  req F-3.10: The engine's properties form edits the selected element's own schema through `update` intents,
    `style.fontSize` among them: set, it overrides the view's own size class for every text part.
  req F-3.11: A `view` node's View field picks any View of the store for it to open, so several nodes may
    share one View; a View that would open the current path again is not offered (`viewOptions`).
  req F-3.12: Style classes are named looks kept beside the Views (`ViewStore.styles`), read from the root model's
    named styles: an element
    naming one in `class` derives its style or line from it. Taking a class drops the element's own
    look; editing a classed element's look edits the class, so every element of it changes together.
    "New class from selection" makes a class of the selected element's look and moves the element onto it.
  req F-3.14: Nodes and links share one `style` object: its common base (`lineStyleFields`: colour and line
    style) is a link's whole style and the start of a node's (`NodeStyle`), which adds fill, tone, frame and text.
    A selection of several kinds edits only the fields every kind declares (`commonSchema` intersects nested
    structs), with one style picker that offers the fill rows only when every element is a node; a class's style
    restyles nodes and links alike, a link taking its common base.
  req F-3.15: A View has ordered layers (`View.layers`, one implicit when it names none) and every node and link
    is on exactly one (`layer`; unset, the bottom one). Elements paint by layer, then by their own order; a hidden
    layer is neither drawn nor hit. The layers panel (`Canvas.Layers`) adds, deletes, merges down, shows or
    hides, reorders and renames layers in place, each as one undo step; new shapes go on the active layer, and the
    properties panel picks an element's layer. Superseded by [F-9.6] and [F-9.7] when Tags land.
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Undo and clipboard
  The engine's own history and clipboard, used where it runs on its own; inside the editor the core's replace them
  ([F-10.3]).
  req F-4.1: Every committed edit — a gesture at pointer-up, a panel edit, a key command, a controller
    command — is one undo entry of field-level patches between the store's heads before and after it.
    Undo writes a field back only while it still holds the value the edit wrote; a field changed since by
    someone else, or an element deleted since, is skipped and the undo reports itself partial.
  req F-4.2: Undo and redo patch a projection's own model (`Projection.model`) with the store, and call
    `restored`, so the compute projection takes its nodes and edges along.
  req F-4.3: Cut / copy / paste use a per-canvas clipboard: fresh ids, links rewired between the pasted
    nodes only, applied as one `batch`. A cut keeps a copy of the records its nodes open, so the paste
    after a cut restores them.
  req F-4.4: One log per canvas document, not per View: undo returns to the View path, isolated Object
    and selection the edit was made in, then reverses it.
  req F-4.5: Consecutive commits with one merge key inside the merge window are one entry, so typing in
    a number field or nudging with the arrow keys undoes in one step.
  req F-4.6: In Composer each commit is recorded as `CanvasOperation.Patch` on the `HistoryTracker`,
    scoped to the canvas object; agents and MCP edit through `CanvasOperation.Apply`, which shares the
    same log and the same inverse.
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Read-only
  req F-5.1: `readonly` lets the view select, pan, zoom and drill, and nothing else.
  req F-5.2: No handle, port, tool or toolbar edit is drawn or reachable, whatever the projection's
    capabilities would allow — the stricter of the two wins.
```

```mdl
feat F-6: Open registry
  req F-6.1: The engine operates on `NodeBase`; a host's type is not a special case.
  req F-6.2: A host composes its registry from the built-ins and its own defs, and its View schema with
    `createViewSchema`.
  req F-6.3: An unknown type still renders rather than throwing, as the core base (`BaseNode`): a node as
    a box showing its `label`, with ports and an editable label, and a link as a straight line. A View
    written by a newer host, or one whose plugin is off, opens.
  req F-6.4: Ports come from the definition; a node may still carry its own.
```

```mdl
feat F-7: Persistence (M4)
  req F-7.1: `createEchoStore(board)` maps nodes to `CanvasBoard.layout.nodes` and links to
    `layout.edges`, deriving `z` from array order on first read, so every board written by the old
    editor opens unchanged.
  req F-7.2: The store is the only seam that changes: `Canvas` takes the ECHO store exactly as it
    takes the memory one.
  req F-7.3: `plugin-conductor`'s `CanvasArticle` renders `Canvas` with the compute projection over
    that store.
  req F-7.4: (planned) react-ui-canvas-compute and plugin-conductor move onto the scene model and the shared
    editor.
```

```mdl
feat F-8: Links between shapes
  req F-8.1: With a link tool a node's BODY is a source like a port is, so two shapes can be joined
    without aiming at their dots; the endpoint carries no port and routing picks the side.
  req F-8.2: A drop resolves the same way: a port claims it only while the pointer is within reach,
    anywhere else on the node binds to the body, and a node whose ports all refuse the end takes a body
    link rather than refusing the drop.
  req F-8.3: Ports are drawn for the node under the pointer alone (and a drop target mid-drag, which
    pointer capture hides from hover) — every node's ports at once is a field of dots over the diagram.
  req F-8.4: A `smart` link is routed, not drawn: a stub leaves each port along its side's normal by
    half a major cell and one segment joins them. Nothing is stored, so it follows the nodes; two ports
    on the same side double back through the middle rather than cutting across their own nodes.
```

```mdl
feat F-9: Views, Objects and Tags
  The containers of the Ontology, and the two panels that show them: Views for the hierarchy, Objects
  for the library.
  req F-9.1: A stored `frame` node reads as `view`, on load, in every store; the next write stores it as
    `view`.
  req F-9.2: Group (⌘G) moves the selected nodes, and the links between them, into an anonymous Object
    (`View.anonymous`) and places one Instance of it over their bounds; a link crossing out of the group
    stays, re-attached to a port of the Instance. There is no separate Compose command.
  req F-9.3: Ungroup (⇧⌘G) puts an Instance's contents back where it drew them, under fresh ids, and
    brings the Object's animation tracks out with them; for an anonymous Object it deletes the
    definition, and a named Object's definition stays.
  req F-9.4: Naming an anonymous Object, or placing it a second time (from the library, a paste of its
    Instance, a duplicate), adds it to the library.
  req F-9.5: Deleting the last node that opens a View, or an anonymous Object's last Instance, deletes
    the record in the same undo step. A record is kept while it is reachable from the root, a
    store-declared root, or a named Object; records already orphaned are dropped on load. There is no
    "Unplaced" bin.
  req F-9.6: (planned) Tags replace layers: hidden or locked is the union over an element's tags, opacity
    the product; layers and `node.layer` migrate to tags on load, and the drawn result is the same
    before and after.
  req F-9.7: (planned) Drawing order is each container's child order alone; layer `z` stops ordering
    anything, and each container is migrated once by sorting on (layer `z`, node `z`).
  req F-9.8: The Views panel shows the full hierarchy, selects on the canvas, opens a View by its drill
    path, restacks by drag and filters by name or type; the canvas selection marks its rows and reveals
    the first without taking focus.
  req F-9.9: An Object cannot contain a View, so an Object stays a drawing rather than a navigable place:
    Group refuses a selection holding a `view` node, and a `view` node created or pasted into an Object's
    definition is refused.
```

```mdl
feat F-10: The 2D viewport in the editor
  req F-10.1: The viewport reads the open scene's 2D layouts as Views, the scene's layout first as the root, a
    composite element as a `view` node over its parts layout's View; 3D layouts are left out, and nothing stores the
    Views.
  req F-10.2: The engine writes its store once per gesture, and the viewport turns that write into one
    `LayoutIntent` per changed layout, a new layout for a View the document has none for (a new portal's), and a
    `ModelIntent` defining the object a new composite places in the root's model, all as one undo step.
  req F-10.3: Inside the editor the engine leaves copy, cut, paste, duplicate, undo, redo, group and ungroup to the
    editor's command table (`HOST_KEYS`); its own history, clipboard and toolbar are not used.
  req F-10.4: Selection, tool, drill path, camera, snapping, axes and guides are the editor's, mirrored into the
    engine's atoms and back; a tool the engine changes itself (a create tool giving way to select) reaches the editor.
  req F-10.5: The 2D Properties form is the engine's own form over the selection's types (ports, link ends, line
    type, a frame's object and role), reached through `CanvasBridge` while the viewport is mounted, and the centre,
    size, rotation, label, layer and style form until it is; it edits a whole selection.
  req F-10.6: Double-clicking a portal drills into it, as the open layout; an Object opens in isolation from the
    Objects section and is never drilled into ([F-1.8]).
```

```mdl
feat F-11: 2D tools, geometry and camera
  req F-11.1: The rail is the engine's palette groups: Select and the hand, then one create group of the node types
    followed by the link types (line, curve, spline, smart), then any further node groups by name; a node tool drags
    a node of its type onto the canvas.
  req F-11.2: Snapping moves by the minor grid and turns by 15 degrees; the n-th paste of a copy lands n major cells
    along each axis.
  req F-11.3: Fit frames the open layout's elements with a major cell of margin; actual size keeps the authored zoom
    and centres the content; the camera bar zooms about the view's centre, up to the engine's largest zoom.
  req F-11.4: The display panel offers the canvas themes as looks, the lines grid, and guides. Every theme is listed
    and can be picked in either mode, in a read-only scene too; a host that stores no theme choice keeps the pick for
    the view alone.
```

```mdl
feat F-12: Axes and guides
  req F-12.1: The axes through the origin are drawn in the grid's colour, stronger, whatever grid is drawn, or none.
  req F-12.2: Guides draw a lattice's cells.
```

```mdl
feat F-13: Diagrams on the canvas
  req F-13.1: The diagram engines' output reaches the canvas as `Layout2D` elements and model objects in one
    `LayoutIntent` batch, each element keeping its DSL identity; `Scene.Command` stays the protocol to the external
    formats (SVG, tldraw, excalidraw).
  req F-13.2: A diagram drawn onto the canvas keeps the canvas's own link routing: a link element stores only its
    endpoints, so the diagram engine's routed bends are dropped; SVG, tldraw and excalidraw output keep them.
  req F-13.3: Diagram objects are flattened into canvas elements until the diagram handler is rewritten on the scene
    model; then (planned) each becomes a relative composite placed by an element, and a link end can name an element
    inside a composite, so an arrow between two objects still attaches to the shape inside it.
```

```mdl
feat F-14: 2D styles and composites
  req F-14.1: `Style2D` is the canvas node style plus the `sketchy` line style, `pattern` and `weight`; diagram
    styles map onto it, so a diagram-styled element can take a catalog style.
  req F-14.2: (planned) The canvas draws `sketchy`, `pattern` and `weight`; the engine's node style has no field for
    them yet, so the viewport drops them.
  req F-14.3: The canvas draws a placed composite at its instance scale; a non-uniform scale draws uniformly,
    letterboxed in the frame.
  req F-14.4: Node fields the element base has no slot for are kept under the element's `props.canvas`, and a node's
    `class` and `style` map to the element's `style` and `overrides` at the viewport's boundary.
```

```mdl
feat F-15: Modes
  req F-15.1: The host's mode is the engine's projection over the Views (freehand when unset; lattice, constrained,
    dynamic and scored as the host passes them), with its generators.
  req F-15.2: A dynamic layout (`arrange` of kind `dynamic`) is its own graph: its shapes are the nodes and its links
    the edges. A shape a person moves is pinned where it lands, and an edit that adds or removes a shape or link
    re-places every unpinned shape by the layout engine, on the layout's lattice when it has one. The lattice is the
    layout's `lattice`.
  req F-15.5: A layout with `constraints` is constrained; its constraints name element ids. A shape an edit moves or
    creates is re-related to its nearest neighbour where it lands, a deleted shape takes its constraints with it, and
    the solver re-places every shape. The new constraints are written by a `layout` intent in the same batch as the
    positions, so one undo restores both. Snapping does not free a constrained layout, and its constraints take
    precedence over a lattice and a dynamic arrangement.
  req F-15.6: Scored mode grades a 2D layout as the canvas draws it: its elements become diagram objects and the
    objective's scorers (the default one unless given) score them.
  req F-15.3: (planned) The viewport draws diagram `arc`, free `curve` and open `polyline` elements.
  req F-15.4: A layout's mode is a rule between an edit and its write, which the editor applies to every write (a
    drag, a command, a paste, the Properties form). A layout with a `lattice` snaps every frame onto whole cells
    while snapping is on and refuses an edit that would overlap another shape's cells; a drag the mode refuses or
    moves is drawn as stored. A host's own projection owns the mode instead.
  req F-15.7: A move follows the pointer, and on each axis a moved shape is held where it would snap (the major grid,
    or its landing cell on a lattice) while within a detent of it, so it clicks into place and out again rather than
    jumping there. A freehand drop lands where the shape is drawn; a lattice drop lands on its cells. On a lattice a smart link
    takes the gutters only while each of its shapes sits on whole cells; a link to a shape between cells (one in
    flight) takes the plain smart route, so its ports change only where the geometry turns and never back and forth.
```

```mdl
feat F-16: The Neu themes
  req F-16.1: The Neu themes (neumorphism) are Neu Light, always light, and Neu Dark, always dark, whatever the app's
    mode. Every Neu shape is the ground colour, and its hue colours only its border and label; a shape with no hue has
    no border of its own.
  req F-16.2: A Neu shape's tone is its height: Inset (cut into the ground, tone 0), Flat (flush with it, tone 1) or
    Raised (tone 2, the default), a raised shape casting a light shadow up and to the left and a dark one down and to
    the right, an inset one the same shadows inside it. Shapes drawn as outlines (polygons, clouds, closed polylines)
    take the same relief as boxes.
  req F-16.3: Selection never changes a Neu shape's relief: a selected shape keeps its tone's surface and shows the
    selection outline and handles.
  req F-16.4: A Neu link is a groove: a channel pressed into the ground, in shade on its upper wall and in light on its
    lower, and its ends are heads of the same channel (an open chevron, a hollow triangle, a ring); a selected or
    hovered groove and its heads are floored in the focus colour.
  req F-16.5: The Neu background is the ground alone, with no grid, and a link caption's backdrop is the ground.
  req F-16.6: The ground and shadow tones are one set of tokens per mode (`NEU_PALETTE`), shared with the 3D Neu
    looks so both dimensions sit on the same ground.
  req F-16.7: A drawing or view that stored a theme, style set or background under its earlier id (`soft`,
    `soft-light`) still draws, as the Neu theme or part that id was renamed to.
```

```mdl
feat F-17: Link ends
  req F-17.1: An arrowhead ends in a point at the connection point on the shape's edge, and the link's line never
    shows past or through it, in every theme, link style and width.
  req F-17.2: The other ends sit the same way: a triangle's point and a circle's far side at the connection point, the
    line stopping under the end.
```

## Planned

- **Diagram objects as relative composites** ([F-13.3]): done with the diagram handler's rewrite onto the scene
  model, together with link ends that name an element inside a composite.
- **The engine's `class`/`style` names** go with the View model they are stored in: they are retired when the engine
  reads elements directly, and are never migrated. Until then node fields with no element slot stay under
  `props.canvas`.

## Rules

Conventions this engine's code must hold that a linter cannot express; each block is discovered by the
agentic-review harness.

```mdl
rule intents-not-coordinates: The surface emits intents; only a projection writes coordinates
  Every model change leaves the view through `projection.apply(intent)`. A component under
  `src/components` must not write `center`, `size`, `z`, `points` or an endpoint into a View, a
  store or a node object — not through `updateView`, not by constructing a `View` and putting it
  back, not by mutating a node it read from the projection. Transient gesture state (the `drag`
  atom's `delta`, `bounds`, `points`, a preview node or link that is never applied) is exactly what
  that state is for and is not a violation. Flag a component that reaches `store`/`updateView`/
  `putView` to change geometry, or that calls `reduceIntent` itself instead of applying an intent.
  Do not flag projections (`src/model/projections/**`, `src/model/projection.ts`), stores, or a host's
  own projection outside this package.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/components/**/*.tsx
    - src/components/**/*.ts
  grep: updateView|putView|reduceIntent
  severity: error
```

```mdl
rule key-chords-live-in-the-table: Every chord is a KEY_BINDINGS entry, matched and labelled from it
  A keyboard chord the engine honours is declared in `src/model/keys.ts` and matched with
  `keyAction` / `matchesBinding`; a label or tooltip naming a shortcut prints it with `shortcutFor`.
  Flag a handler comparing `event.key` to a literal, or a UI string containing a chord written out
  ('⌘Z', 'Ctrl+C', 'Press G'), anywhere under `src/components`. Tool letters are the one exception:
  they live on the registry entries as `NodeDef.key` / `LinkDef.key` and are matched by the palette
  through `isToolKey`. Do not flag `keys.ts` itself or its test.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/components/**/*.tsx
    - src/components/**/*.ts
  grep: event\.key|\.key ===|⌘|Ctrl\+|Cmd\+
  severity: error
```

```mdl
rule scene-bounds-are-derived: Never store a View's bounds
  A View's extent is the union of its node frames, padded and grown to the major grid, computed where
  it is needed (`utils/hit.ts`, `sceneBounds`). Do not add a bounds, extent, width or height field to
  `View`, cache one on a store, or persist one — a stored extent is a second source of truth that
  goes stale on every move and gives an infinite canvas an edge (decision 6). Flag a `View` literal,
  schema or ECHO record carrying such a field. A `Bounds` VALUE passed around (a portal frame, a
  marquee, a resize) is not a stored bound and is not a violation.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/model/**/*.ts
    - src/utils/**/*.ts
  severity: error
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
scenario T-1: Drill in and back out
  given: the Nested story, a portal visible in the root View
  when: double-clicking the portal, then pressing Escape
  then:
    - the camera animates into the portal and stops at or before 1:1 for the child's content
    - the grid lines either side of the swap are drawn at the same spacing and weight
    - the breadcrumbs gain a level, then lose it again; the camera returns to where it left
```

```mdl
scenario T-2: A link survives its node moving
  given: two nodes joined by a curve with no explicit ports
  when: dragging one node across the View
  then: the link re-pairs to the closest appropriate ports on every frame, and still leaves each port
    along its side's normal
```

```mdl
scenario T-3: A free-ended link
  given: the Freehand story with the line tool active
  when: dragging on empty canvas, away from every node
  then: a link with two free `{point}` ends is created; dragging one end onto a node's port attaches it
```

```mdl
scenario T-4: Read-only
  given: the Readonly story
  when: clicking a node, then attempting to drag it, resize it, or press Delete
  then: the node is selected, no handle or port is drawn, and the View is unchanged
```

```mdl
scenario T-5: Undo across a projection
  given: the Constrained story
  when: moving a node (which the projection rewrites into constraints), then pressing ⌘Z
  then: both the positioned View and the constraint list return to their previous state in one step
```

```mdl
scenario T-6: An existing board opens unchanged (M4)
  given: a `CanvasBoard` written by `react-ui-canvas-editor`, with nodes and edges in `layout`
  when: opening it in the plugin on the scene engine
  then: every node keeps its position and size, every edge its ends, and the z-order matches the array
    order it was stored in
```

## QA

Executable tests over the **storybook**, not the app: the engine ships no plugin of its own, and the
stories are the surface a host composes. Run `moon run storybook-react:serve` (9009) or the e2e
storybook (9006); `moon run react-ui-canvas:e2e` runs the automated subset.

```mdl
test QA-1: The editor surface end to end
  desc: |
    The gestures a host inherits by rendering `Canvas`. Ten of these steps are automated in
    `src/playwright/scene.spec.ts` over the Freehand and Readonly stories; the rest are human because
    they judge motion (the drill-in animation, grid continuity) rather than a DOM state.
  actors: human, agent
  covers: [F-1.1, F-1.2, F-2.1, F-2.2, F-3.1, F-3.2, F-3.3, F-5.1, T-1, T-3, T-4]
  status: partially-automated
  given:
    - a storybook serving `ui/react-ui-canvas/Canvas`
  steps:
    - name: Open Freehand
      do: Open the Freehand story at 1400×800.
      expect: four nodes and three links, fitted; the toolbar reads a zoom, a pointer and depth 0
    - name: Marquee
      do: Drag on empty canvas across two nodes, then repeat holding Alt.
      expect: the first drag selects both; the Alt drag removes them from the selection
    - name: Move and snap
      do: Drag a node with snap on (G toggles it).
      expect: the node follows the pointer and holds on a major grid line on each axis that comes within the detent of one; the readout follows the pointer
    - name: Draw a link
      do: Hover a node, drag from one of its ports onto another node.
      expect: a band follows the pointer, the target's ports show, and a link is created on release
    - name: Drill in
      do: Switch to the Nested story and double-click a portal.
      expect: the camera animates in, stops at natural text size, and the grid does not jump at the swap
    - name: Drill out
      do: Press Escape.
      expect: the camera returns to the parent, the breadcrumbs lose a level
    - name: Read-only
      do: Open the Readonly story and try to drag a node.
      expect: it selects, draws no handles, and does not move
  after:
    - name: None
      do: Stories hold no persistent state; reload to reset.
      expect: the fixture is back
```

```mdl
test QA-2: Persistence round trip (M4)
  desc: |
    The one thing stories cannot cover, because it needs a space: that a board written by the old
    editor opens unchanged on the engine, and that an edit made on the engine survives a reload.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-7.1, F-7.2, F-7.3, T-6]
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity and an open space
    - a CanvasBoard created by the previous editor, with at least two nodes and one edge
  steps:
    - name: Open it
      do: Open the board in Composer.
      expect: every node is where it was, every edge joins the same two nodes
    - name: Edit
      do: Move a node, add one, and join them.
      expect: the changes appear immediately
    - name: Reload
      do: Reload the app and reopen the board.
      expect: the move, the new node and the new link are all still there
```

## Changelog

```mdl
decision D-1: 2D moves fully onto the shared model and edits (`LayoutIntent`, the shared store); react-ui-canvas's own View, Node and Link model, its `Intent` and its `ViewStore` are retired, and its lattice, constrained, dynamic and scored modes are ported.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-10, F-15]
```

```mdl
decision D-2: Every field react-ui-canvas records carry (ports, path and polyline points, view and portal links, z order, lattice cells, constraint and dynamic models, generators, diagram shapes) becomes a typed field of `Element2D`, or of `Layout2D` where it belongs to the layout, not an untyped `props` entry.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-14.4]
```

```mdl
decision D-3: Diagrams have one text language and one data model: `.dx` semantic statements and scene statements both target `@dxos/scene`; the layout engines output `Layout2D` elements and model objects as one `LayoutIntent` batch, each element keeping its DSL identity in a typed field; `@dxos/diagram`'s own scene model is retired as a data model, the illustrator's SVG renderer draws `Layout2D`, and the canvas builder's translation layer disappears.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-13.1]
```

```mdl
decision D-4: Constrained and dynamic models are normalised onto the layout's elements: constraints reference element ids, graph edges are link elements, and overlay positions are element centres. The 2D viewport gets new draw code for diagram `arc`, free `curve` and open `polyline` elements.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.2, F-15.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-5: react-ui-canvas-compute, and plugin-conductor through it, move onto the shared model and editor too: one data structure everywhere, with no frozen copy of the old model.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-7.4]
```

```mdl
decision D-6: 2D has one style vocabulary: `Style2D` is the canvas node style extended with a `sketchy` line style, `pattern` and `weight` (which scales stroke and text). Diagram styles map onto it, and the diagram `color`, `fill`, `stroke` and `weight` fields disappear from elements, so diagram-styled elements can take catalog styles.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-14.1, F-14.2]
```

```mdl
decision D-7: A diagram drawn onto the 2D canvas keeps the canvas's own link routing: link elements store only their endpoints, so the diagram engine's routed bends are dropped, as the legacy canvas did; SVG, tldraw and excalidraw output keep them.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-13.2]
  rejected:
    - An optional route on link elements, drawn while both ends stay put, as the diagram's connector keeps one.
```

```mdl
decision D-8: In 2D the axes are drawn in a stronger grid colour.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-12.1]
```

```mdl
decision D-9: Until the engine is rewritten over `Element2D`, the 2D viewport keeps the engine's View model as an in-memory working form: it reads the layouts into Views, and turns each engine store write into intents; nothing stores the Views. The engine still runs on its own for legacy canvases and the compute board, so its requirements F-1 to F-9 stay. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-10.1, F-10.2, F-4, F-7]
  why: The engine's gestures, portals, links and modes are what 2D is; reading and writing it through the scene model put 2D on the shared document, intents and history without rewriting them.
  rejected:
    - Rewriting the engine over `Element2D` first, which would have held back the shared editor until every engine feature was ported.
```

```mdl
decision D-10: Node fields the element base has no slot for stay under the element's `props.canvas`, and `class` and `style` map to `style` and `overrides` at the viewport's boundary, until the engine's own rename. Fills in [D-2].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-14.4]
  why: Moving the fields first needs the engine's rename, and its `InstanceNode.overrides` and `fit` collide with the element base's fields of those names.
```

```mdl
decision D-11: `Scene.Command` stays the protocol between the diagram engines and the external formats (SVG, tldraw, excalidraw); the canvas builder lands the commands on a canvas as one `LayoutIntent` batch. Fills in [D-3].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-13.1]
  why: The commands feed four backends, not only the canvas; retiring them as a data model does not require retiring the protocol.
  rejected:
    - New converters from `Layout2D` to tldraw, excalidraw and SVG, with read-back.
```

```mdl
decision D-12: Inside the editor, the engine leaves its host keys to the editor's command table, the editor's selection, tool, drill path, camera and display are mirrored into the engine both ways, and the 2D Properties form is the engine's own form, reached through a bridge while the viewport is mounted. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-10.3, F-10.4, F-10.5, Viewport2D, CanvasBridge]
  why: One owner for each shortcut and each piece of view state; the engine's form already edits every node type's own fields, which a generic form cannot.
```

```mdl
decision D-13: In 2D, snapping moves by the minor grid and turns by 15 degrees, a paste steps a major cell, fit leaves a major cell of margin, and the camera bar zooms about the view's centre. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-11.2, F-11.3]
  why: These are the steps the canvas has always used, so 2D behaves as it did before the shared editor.
```

```mdl
decision D-14: The canvas draws a placed composite at its instance scale, and a non-uniform scale draws uniformly, letterboxed in the frame. Fills in org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas#D-19.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-14.3]
  why: The engine's portal and instance views scale their content uniformly; a letterbox keeps the parts undistorted.
```

```mdl
decision D-15: The 2D rail is the engine's palette groups, a node tool dragging a node of its type onto the canvas, and the display panel offers the canvas themes, the lines grid and guides. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-11.1, F-11.4]
  why: The palette's grouping and drag-to-place were how 2D created shapes before the shared rail, and the canvas themes are 2D's looks.
```

```mdl
decision D-16: Diagram objects become relative composites placed by elements, and a link end can name an element inside a composite, so an arrow between two objects attaches to the shape inside it rather than to the composite's frame; this is done with the diagram handler's rewrite onto the scene model.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-13.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-17: The engine's `class`/`style` field names are not migrated: they are retired together with the View model that stores them, when the engine reads elements directly.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-14]
```

```mdl
decision D-18: A layout's mode is a rule the editor applies between every edit and its write (`DimensionConfig.constrain`), and 2D's lattice is selected by the layout's own `lattice` field while snapping is on; a host that passes its own projection keeps the mode. Fills in [D-1] and [D-4].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.4]
  why: Applying the rule at the write, not only in the engine's drag, holds commands, pastes and form edits to the lattice too, and reading it from the layout lets it travel with the drawing.
  rejected: A rule inside the 2D viewport only, which a paste or a Properties edit would bypass.
```

```mdl
decision D-19: A 2D layout is dynamic when its `arrange` is of kind `dynamic`: its shapes and links are the graph, a moved shape is pinned (its centre is the overlay), and a change to the graph re-places the unpinned shapes. Constrained mode keeps its constraints in the layout's `constraints` and is planned until a layout field can be written as one undoable step with the positions. Fills in [D-4].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.2, F-15.5]
  why: `arrange` already says how a layout places elements with no position, and a person's move already pins an element of an arranged layout, so the graph needs no second copy of the elements.
  rejected: A separate `graph` field on the layout, which would duplicate the links and centres the elements hold.
```

```mdl
decision D-20: Scored mode reads a 2D layout through the same read model the 2D viewport draws from, so a layout scores exactly as it looks. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.6]
  why: The viewport still reads layouts through the canvas adapter, so scoring through it measures what the person sees, including routed links.
  rejected: A second element-to-diagram conversion for scoring, which could drift from what the canvas draws.
```

```mdl
decision D-21: Constraints are written through a layout-field intent, undone together with the elements (R-12 option 1).
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.5]
```

```mdl
decision D-22: The layout-field intent is `{ kind: 'layout', patch }`, a `LayoutIntent` whose patch holds the 2D layout fields a mode owns (`lattice`, `constraints`): a key with a value sets the field, a key set to `undefined` unsets it, an absent key leaves it, and a batch's patches merge in order. The shared reducer applies it with the element intents, the canvas's layout writer writes the changed fields in the same change, and the layout's versioned source covers those fields, so undo and redo restore them with the elements. Fills in [D-21].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.5]
  why: One patch over named layout fields lets lattice (and later layers) use the same write path without a new intent kind each, and keeping it inside `LayoutIntent` gives it the editor's existing recording, undo and sharing for free.
  rejected:
    - A path-and-value update on the layout (like the element `update`), which loses the field types.
    - One intent kind per field (`constraints`, `lattice`), which grows the vocabulary with every mode.
```

```mdl
decision D-23: A 2D layout is constrained when its `constraints` field is set, even to an empty list; constrained mode then takes precedence over the layout's lattice and dynamic arrangement and runs whether or not snapping is on, and an edit that moves, creates or deletes no shape passes through unchanged with no layout patch. Fills in [D-21] and [D-19].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.5]
  why: The solver owns every shape's position, so a second mode or turning snapping off would only produce positions the next constrained edit overwrites; an empty list is a constrained layout whose first drop starts its relations.
  rejected:
    - A separate mode field naming the layout's mode, which would duplicate what the fields already say.
```

```mdl
decision D-24: Dragging a shape on a lattice must not make its links flicker between the layout under the pointer and the settled layout.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.7]
```

```mdl
decision D-25: A move on a lattice previews its landing cells rather than following the pointer. Fills in [D-24].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.7]
  why: A shape between cells re-routes its gutter links on every pixel, since two port pairs tie on bends and the nearer one flips along a diagonal; drawing the landing changes the drawing only when the cell changes, and shows what the drop will do.
  rejected:
    - Keeping each link's ports for the length of the drag until another pair bends strictly less, which leaves the jump between the pointer's layout and the settled one when the pointer rests.
    - Plain routing for links on a moving shape until the drop, which changes the link's shape on release.
```

```mdl
decision D-26: Shapes are dragged live: a move follows the pointer on a lattice too.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  supersedes: D-25
  affects: [F-15.7]
```

```mdl
decision D-27: A smart link takes the gutters only while each of its shapes sits exactly on whole cells; otherwise it takes the plain smart route and stays out of the gutter lanes. Fills in [D-24] and [D-26].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.7]
  why: Gutter routing assumes shapes on cells; for a shape between them two port pairs tie on bends and the nearer one flips every few pixels, while the plain route changes its ports only where the geometry turns. Reading it from the geometry, not from the drag, keeps the canvas layer and the selection overlay on the same route without passing the drag down.
  rejected:
    - Keeping each link's ports for the length of the drag until another pair bends strictly less, which still jumps when the gutter route's bend count changes under a moving shape.
```

```mdl
decision D-28: Snapping is a detent, not a timed settle: a moved shape snaps to where it lands when it comes close and snaps out once it leaves that threshold.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.7]
```

```mdl
decision D-29: The detent is per axis and 8 screen pixels wide, applied by the move's offset to the major grid and by the move preview to the projection's landing cell; a freehand drop lands where the shape is drawn. Fills in [D-28].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-15.7]
  why: Per axis lets a shape run along a line while it slides freely across it, and a screen-sized detent feels the same at every zoom. The freehand offset is what the drop applies, so detenting it there keeps the drop where the shape is drawn; a lattice keeps every shape on cells, so its drop still lands on them.
  rejected:
    - One detent on the distance to the landing point, which drops a shape off a line it is sliding along as soon as it moves away across it.
    - Snapping a freehand drop to the major grid wherever it is released, which jumps on release.
```

```mdl
decision D-30: Approve [D-29].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
```

```mdl
decision D-31: Smart links are the default for every fixture and every generated diagram, the circuit fixtures included.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [Link]
```

```mdl
decision D-32: A link that names no type reads as `smart` wherever a stored layout is drawn (a scene layout, a compute board, a dynamic layout), and the illustrator's DSL arrows are written as smart links whether or not they are bound. Fills in [D-31].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [Link]
  why: Generated diagrams and fixtures mostly store no link type, so changing the default reaches them all at once; a stored type still wins, so a link someone chose by hand keeps its shape.
  rejected:
    - Writing `smart` into each fixture and generator while the absent type still reads as a line, which leaves every existing generated document and any new generator on the old default.
```

```mdl
decision D-33: Add a neumorphism (Soft) theme to the 2D canvas, in light and dark mode. The hue is an accent only: shapes are the ground colour, raised by soft dual shadows (a light shadow up and to the left, a dark one down and to the right), and the element's hue colours only the border and label.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.1, F-16.2]
```

```mdl
decision D-34: A selected soft shape is pressed in (inner shadows instead of raised), and the usual selection outline and handles stay.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-35: Soft links are grooves: channels pressed into the ground, with an inset shadow either side, and arrowheads in the accent hue.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.4]
```

```mdl
decision D-36: The 2D Soft theme and the 3D Clay theme share the decision and, where possible, the implementation: the soft ground and body tones for light and dark, and the shadow parameters, live in one shared place that the 3D theme reads, so both have the same ground colour in each mode.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.6]
```

```mdl
decision D-37: The soft tokens are `SOFT_PALETTE` (ground #e3e7ee light and #2a2e35 dark, a groove floor a step darker, a white highlight and a blue-grey or black shade) and `SOFT_RELIEF` (raised 6px offset and 14px blur, inset 4 and 8, pressed 6 and 12, in scene px), published to the canvas as CSS variables whose colours are `light-dark()` pairs. Fills in [D-33] and [D-36].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.1, F-16.6]
  why: The 3D renderer needs the ground as a hex value, so the TypeScript constant is the source; `light-dark()` lets one set of classes follow the app's mode and a theme's forced mode without a class per tone. The dark ground sits well above black so its dark shadow still reads.
  rejected:
    - Literal Tailwind classes per tone, which would copy every value into class names the 3D renderer cannot read.
    - A theme stylesheet, which would split the values from the TypeScript the 3D theme imports.
```

```mdl
decision D-38: The soft style set's variants are Flat, Inset and Raised, in tone order, Raised the default; shapes have a 1px border and large (`xl`, rounded `2xl`) corners. Fills in [D-33].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.2]
  why: Like every set, tone 0 is the plainest (flush with the ground); a node with no tone draws raised, the look the theme is named for; soft relief reads best on generous corners.
  rejected:
    - A fourth, higher Raised variant, which would add a shadow step the theme does not need.
```

```mdl
decision D-39: A selected soft shape swaps its surface for a pressed one, an inner shadow deeper than Inset's, so a selected Inset shape still sinks and a Flat one goes in. Fills in [D-34].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.3]
  why: The frame already knows its selection, so the surface table carries the pressed classes beside the resting ones; other sets have none and keep their fill.
```

```mdl
decision D-40: A groove is three copies of the link's path: one nudged up and to the left in the shade colour, one nudged down and to the right in the highlight colour, each a quarter of the groove's width, under a floor a step darker than the ground; the soft set draws grooves 8px wide, and a selected or hovered groove floors in the focus colour. Fills in [D-35].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.4]
  why: An SVG stroke can be neither offset nor inset-shadowed; nudged copies cost three plain paths, where a blur filter per link would be far dearer on a large drawing. A groove takes no hue, so selection needs a fill of its own.
  rejected:
    - An SVG drop-shadow filter per link.
```

```mdl
decision D-41: The soft background has no grid, and a link caption's backdrop and a hollow end marker's inside are the ground rather than the canvas's surface. Fills in [D-33].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.5]
  why: The shapes' relief is the theme's only texture, and a grid reads as print over it; a white caption backdrop would break the single-tone ground. The display panel's grid buttons still draw one on request.
```

```mdl
decision D-42: Do not show selected soft shapes as indented; indentation is a style choice, carried by one of the tone values.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  supersedes: D-34
  affects: [F-16.2, F-16.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-43: A selected soft shape keeps its tone's surface; the pressed relief and its CSS variable are removed, and only the selection outline and handles show selection. Fills in [D-42].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  supersedes: D-39
  affects: [F-16.3]
  why: With selection no longer a relief, nothing else used the pressed shadow, so keeping it would leave a token no surface draws.
```

```mdl
decision D-44: The soft style set's variants are Inset, Flat and Raised, in tone order (tones 0, 1 and 2), Raised the default; shapes have a 1px border and large (`xl`, rounded `2xl`) corners. Fills in [D-33] and [D-42].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  supersedes: D-38
  affects: [F-16.2]
  why: The tone reads as height out of the ground, so the scale is monotonic like the other sets' fills (clear to solid); the default stays at index 2, so a shape given the flat and classic sets' default tone explicitly draws raised here too, and tone 3 draws as the last, raised.
  rejected:
    - Flat, Inset, Raised (the earlier order), which puts the cut-in shape between two above it.
    - Flat, Raised, Inset (by emphasis), which makes the deepest relief the strongest tone though it recedes.
```

```mdl
decision D-45: A soft shape drawn as an outline gets its relief from an SVG layer per shape: its outline in the ground colour under one filter that merges two blurred, offset copies of its alpha (the highlight up-left, the shade down-right). Raised, the layer sits beside the frame, which clips, so the shadow falls outside; inset, it draws the fill itself with the shadows inside, over which the masked border draws. Fills in [D-33] and [D-42].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.2]
  why: An outline shape is a box masked to its path, which masks away any box-shadow; the filter matches the box-shadow's offset and blur (deviation half the blur) and runs only on outline shapes in a soft theme.
  rejected:
    - A CSS drop-shadow on the masked layers, which the mask clips and which has no inset form.
    - Unblurred offset copies of the mask, which draw hard steps rather than soft relief.
```

```mdl
decision D-46: Create light-mode versions of the soft themes alongside the ones that follow the app.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.7]
```

```mdl
decision D-47: Soft Light (`soft-light`) is a theme of its own: the soft style set and background with mode `light`, listed after Soft in the picker and the stories. Fills in [D-46].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.7]
  why: A theme's `mode` is how Paper and Film already hold a look light or dark, so the picker shows its sun and every host that stores a theme id stores it unchanged.
  rejected:
    - A separate light-mode switch on the theme, which no host stores and the picker has no place for.
```

```mdl
decision D-48: Every theme is always selectable, in read-only scenes and in hosts that store no theme choice.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-11.4]
```

```mdl
decision D-49: The theme picker is a way of viewing, like the axes toggle, so read-only no longer disables it, and it lists every built-in theme whatever the app's mode. A host that takes the pick stores it as before; one that does not keeps it in the view's memory, over the host's theme, until the view unmounts. The 2D canvas and the shared scene editor (2D and 3D) work the same way. Fills in [D-48].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-11.4]
  why: A read-only viewer still chooses how to look at a drawing; holding the pick in memory needs no host change and cannot outlive the view in a host that never meant to store it.
  rejected:
    - Browser storage per drawing, which would persist a choice the host never offered to keep.
    - Hiding themes that force the other mode, which would hide Paper and Film from half the users.
```

```mdl
decision D-50: Soft-theme arrowheads are drawn in the link's groove style.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.4]
```

```mdl
decision D-51: A groove's ends are scene paths stroked in the track's three layers, as wide as the track, every lip drawn before any floor, so track and head read as one channel: an open chevron for an arrow, a hollow triangle and a ring. The heads take no hue, as the track takes none, and floor in the focus colour with it when selected or hovered. Fills in [D-50], replacing the accent-hue arrowheads of [D-35].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.4]
  why: An SVG marker draws in its own rotated frame, so lip copies nudged inside it would turn with the link while the track's lips stay fixed in the scene; paths in the scene frame share the track's nudge and cost three plain paths per link, no filter. The canvas's markers are arrow, triangle and circle only, so there is no diamond or bar to draw.
  rejected:
    - Groove markers (`<marker>` with nudged copies), whose light and shade would rotate with each link.
    - A hued head on a hueless track, which reads as a separate part rather than the end of the channel.
```

```mdl
decision D-52: An arrowhead ends in a point at the connection point, and the link's line never shows past or through it.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-17.1, F-17.2]
```

```mdl
decision D-53: A pointed end is stroked with a mitred join and placed so the mitre's point, not its vertex, is the connection point; a circle sits with its far side there. The line is cut along its route (curves split, not flattened) to where the end covers it: where its edges meet an open arrow's inner edges (half its width further for a round cap), at a triangle's base, at a circle's centre; a groove's track stops at its chevron's vertex, its triangle's base or its ring's near side, where the head's channel covers its round cap. This holds for every link style and width. 3D links draw no heads, so the rule has nothing to apply to there. Fills in [D-52].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-17.1, F-17.2]
  why: A round join or cap reaches half the stroke past its vertex, so a rounded tip either stops short of the edge or runs into the shape; cutting the line under the end is the one way an open arrow hides it, and the cut lengths depend only on the end, the stroke and the line's width, so they are pure functions tested on their own.
  rejected:
    - Filled arrowheads, which would hide the line but change the open arrow's look in every theme.
    - Drawing the line to the connection point under an opaque end, which an open arrow cannot cover.
```

```mdl
decision D-54: The neumorphism themes are named Neu Light and Neu Dark, each always in its mode, in 2D and 3D.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  supersedes: D-46
  affects: [F-16.1, F-16.6]
```

```mdl
decision D-55: The ids follow the names: themes `neu-light` and `neu-dark` (Light listed first), style set and background `neu` named Neu, surfaces `neu-inset`, `neu-flat` and `neu-raised`, CSS variables `--dx-neu-*`, and the tokens `NEU_PALETTE`, `NEU_RELIEF` and `neuVars` in `model/neu.ts`; no follows-the-app Neu theme remains. Fills in [D-54].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  supersedes: D-47
  affects: [F-16.1, F-16.6]
  why: Code, stored ids and the picker naming one thing keeps a search for the theme finding all of it; the repo keeps no aliases, so the old names go everywhere at once.
  rejected:
    - Renaming the display names only, which leaves `soft` ids and `SOFT_*` tokens behind a theme no longer called Soft.
```

```mdl
decision D-56: A stored id from before the rename resolves through one table, `RENAMED_LOOK_IDS` (`soft` to `neu-dark`, `soft-light` to `neu-light`, style set and background `soft` to `neu`), consulted by `resolveTheme`, `resolveStyleSet` and `resolveBackground` only when the registry has no entry by that id, and by plugin-canvas's picker through `currentLookId`. Fills in [D-54].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-16.7]
  why: Drawings and settings store theme ids as plain strings, and users may already have picked Soft; the old Soft followed the app, so `soft` maps to Neu Dark as its closest fixed-mode successor, and a host's own entry under an old id still wins.
  rejected:
    - Keeping `soft` and `soft-light` as registered alias themes, which would list them in the picker and is a shim the repo forbids.
    - Migrating stored ids, which would write to every drawing that names one and still miss offline peers.
```

